WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.732 --> 00:00:02.035
- The challenge should be to try and play

00:00:02.035 --> 00:00:04.470
as diverse characters as possible.

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:05.793
It would get boring for the audience

00:00:05.793 --> 00:00:07.356
and for the actor if you played

00:00:07.356 --> 00:00:09.703
characters that were similar all the time.

00:00:09.703 --> 00:00:12.243
What I want is to work with
people with strong visions.

00:00:12.243 --> 00:00:14.710
They do this crazy thing
now more for boys and girls,

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:16.753
they do this chemistry test.

00:00:16.753 --> 00:00:18.835
That's the most absurd
thing in the world to me.

00:00:18.835 --> 00:00:20.492
I don't know, sometimes you feel

00:00:20.492 --> 00:00:22.791
like a total waste of space really

00:00:22.791 --> 00:00:24.887
when you look at people
who have proper jobs.

00:00:24.887 --> 00:00:26.880
And then you go, "What am I doing?"

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:28.455
- Hello Believe Nation, it's Evan.

00:00:28.455 --> 00:00:31.272
My one word is believe
and I believe in people

00:00:31.272 --> 00:00:33.121
more than they believe in themselves

00:00:33.121 --> 00:00:34.888
and my sincere hope is that if you see

00:00:34.888 --> 00:00:37.581
in yourself what I see in you,

00:00:37.581 --> 00:00:39.532
you'll be able to change the planet.

00:00:39.532 --> 00:00:40.629
So to help you on your journey,

00:00:40.629 --> 00:00:42.986
today we're going to learn
from actor Cillian Murphy

00:00:42.986 --> 00:00:45.982
and my take on his top
ten rules to success.

00:00:45.982 --> 00:00:47.862
Rule number seven is my personal favorite

00:00:47.862 --> 00:00:50.707
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:50.707 --> 00:00:52.271
Also as you're watching,
if you hear something

00:00:52.271 --> 00:00:53.543
that really resonates with you,

00:00:53.543 --> 00:00:55.127
please leave it down in the comments below

00:00:55.127 --> 00:00:56.509
and put quotes around it so other people

00:00:56.509 --> 00:00:58.398
can be inspired by it as well.

00:00:58.398 --> 00:01:00.215
And if you leave it within
the first couple hours

00:01:00.215 --> 00:01:01.819
of this video going
live, you have a chance

00:01:01.819 --> 00:01:04.532
to win one of two daily prizes.

00:01:04.532 --> 00:01:07.280
(air swooshes)

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:10.030
(dramatic music)

00:01:17.711 --> 00:01:19.418
- The challenge should be to try and play

00:01:19.418 --> 00:01:21.421
as diverse characters as possible.

00:01:21.421 --> 00:01:22.750
It would get boring for the audience

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:24.273
and for the actor if you played

00:01:24.273 --> 00:01:26.549
characters that were similar all the time.

00:01:26.549 --> 00:01:29.707
- [Bird] Hop one, hop
two, hop one, two, three.

00:01:29.707 --> 00:01:32.392
Tish tish tish, flap flap, flap.

00:01:32.392 --> 00:01:34.038
- We're actors, we're
supposed to be able to

00:01:34.038 --> 00:01:37.756
inhabit different
characters, that's our job.

00:01:37.756 --> 00:01:39.756
That's what acting should be surely.

00:01:39.756 --> 00:01:42.266
It could be action flick, ain't it?

00:01:42.266 --> 00:01:44.599
(pop music)

00:01:46.115 --> 00:01:49.174
I'm Cillian Murphy and
I'm an actor, I suppose.

00:01:49.174 --> 00:01:50.678
- Cillian I've also heard you say that

00:01:50.678 --> 00:01:52.913
one of your happiest places is on set

00:01:52.913 --> 00:01:54.760
when you hear the word action.

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:55.867
Why?

00:01:55.867 --> 00:01:57.232
- I mean if I'm in the film obviously.

00:01:57.232 --> 00:01:58.522
(laughter)

00:01:58.522 --> 00:02:00.038
- We're like hiding, watching.

00:02:00.038 --> 00:02:04.183
- Otherwise it's just awkward
for everyone involved.

00:02:04.183 --> 00:02:05.382
- Isn't that Cillian Murphy?

00:02:05.382 --> 00:02:06.711
What's he doing here?

00:02:06.711 --> 00:02:08.111
He's just watching.

00:02:08.111 --> 00:02:10.839
- He'll leave soon.
- He's happy now.

00:02:10.839 --> 00:02:11.672
- Yeah well it is.

00:02:11.672 --> 00:02:14.583
It's that thing where that's the moment

00:02:14.583 --> 00:02:17.072
you've been looking forward
to building up towards.

00:02:17.072 --> 00:02:19.655
And it is true to say that time

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:24.071
kind of slows down and
you stop sensing things

00:02:24.071 --> 00:02:27.238
in kind of a normal way and I love it.

00:02:28.784 --> 00:02:30.767
It's my favorite part of the whole thing,

00:02:30.767 --> 00:02:32.908
it's the thing you kind of live for.

00:02:32.908 --> 00:02:36.232
- What was the hardest scene or moment

00:02:36.232 --> 00:02:39.725
for you as an actor
this season on the show?

00:02:39.725 --> 00:02:41.706
- The whole thing is challenging due to

00:02:41.706 --> 00:02:44.429
the nature of the schedule.

00:02:44.429 --> 00:02:46.298
It's a very talk-y show,
there's a lot of dialogue

00:02:46.298 --> 00:02:49.798
and there's a lot of big scenes for Tommy.

00:02:52.954 --> 00:02:54.710
This season particularly,
there was a lot of

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:56.823
trauma that he had to endure.

00:02:56.823 --> 00:02:58.378
I don't know man, I love doing it.

00:02:58.378 --> 00:03:02.237
I love the big scenes, I love
working with Helen McCrorey,

00:03:02.237 --> 00:03:03.434
I love working with Paul Anderson,

00:03:03.434 --> 00:03:05.568
I love working with Tom Hardy.

00:03:05.568 --> 00:03:09.735
I relish those big scenes,
those confrontational scenes,

00:03:11.196 --> 00:03:14.336
they're very very satisfying for an actor

00:03:14.336 --> 00:03:17.669
when you get that caliber of acting ops.

00:03:21.983 --> 00:03:24.054
You always go home from work going,

00:03:24.054 --> 00:03:26.919
"Wow, I went to work today."

00:03:26.919 --> 00:03:29.409
- So this would be, I'm
trying to think now,

00:03:29.409 --> 00:03:31.359
is this your third or fourth
film with Christopher Nolan?

00:03:31.359 --> 00:03:32.648
- Well people are saying fifth

00:03:32.648 --> 00:03:37.453
but I only pop up in the
Batman thing a little bit.

00:03:37.453 --> 00:03:41.264
What you want as an actor,
or certainly what I want

00:03:41.264 --> 00:03:43.977
is to work with people
with strong visions.

00:03:43.977 --> 00:03:45.541
And you can't find anybody with a stronger

00:03:45.541 --> 00:03:48.519
vision than Christopher Nolan.

00:03:48.519 --> 00:03:50.692
He's written, produced
and directed this thing

00:03:50.692 --> 00:03:53.025
and it's kind of phenomenal.

00:03:53.984 --> 00:03:55.192
It is still collaborative.

00:03:55.192 --> 00:03:56.672
We spent a lot of time on the phone

00:03:56.672 --> 00:03:57.603
talking about this character,

00:03:57.603 --> 00:04:00.010
talking about how to pitch it and he just

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:02.277
kind of left me off to
go and do my research

00:04:02.277 --> 00:04:04.377
and then bring my research to him and say,

00:04:04.377 --> 00:04:05.636
"Look, I thought this, this and this

00:04:05.636 --> 00:04:07.640
"might be appropriate,
so what do you think?"

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:10.464
So yeah, there is absolutely
scope to collaborate

00:04:10.464 --> 00:04:13.228
but you want to work
with someone who is that.

00:04:13.228 --> 00:04:14.061
- [Interviewer] That'll guide you?

00:04:14.061 --> 00:04:17.061
- Yeah or just has, he knows exactly

00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:21.221
every frame of the film
as he's shooting it

00:04:21.221 --> 00:04:23.537
which is so comforting.

00:04:23.537 --> 00:04:27.906
It's a real privilege to
work with him every time.

00:04:27.906 --> 00:04:29.482
There's no argument that he's one of the

00:04:29.482 --> 00:04:31.322
greatest directors around at the moment.

00:04:31.322 --> 00:04:32.611
I think everyone accepts that.

00:04:32.611 --> 00:04:35.223
So as an actor, selfishly you want to work

00:04:35.223 --> 00:04:38.666
with the best people and
he's definitely one of those.

00:04:38.666 --> 00:04:40.860
But every relationship between a director

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:44.438
and an actor should be collaborative.

00:04:44.438 --> 00:04:46.125
They're the boss and they're the leader

00:04:46.125 --> 00:04:50.037
but it has to be an exchange
of ideas all the way.

00:04:50.037 --> 00:04:52.159
I just was realizing that I was really

00:04:52.159 --> 00:04:54.556
into performing and I wasn't quite sure.

00:04:54.556 --> 00:04:55.389
Live performance.

00:04:55.389 --> 00:04:57.114
I really like playing
the guitar and singing

00:04:57.114 --> 00:05:00.094
and I really like acting on stage.

00:05:00.094 --> 00:05:01.313
This director of a theater company

00:05:01.313 --> 00:05:03.421
came in and worked with us for

00:05:03.421 --> 00:05:04.254
over the course of a few months

00:05:04.254 --> 00:05:06.958
and I loved it and I thought he was the

00:05:06.958 --> 00:05:09.883
coolest guy ever, I developed
a huge man crush on him.

00:05:09.883 --> 00:05:11.664
And then I'd see him out in Cork drinking

00:05:11.664 --> 00:05:13.722
in pubs and things and I was like,

00:05:13.722 --> 00:05:15.573
"You got to give me an
audition for a play."

00:05:15.573 --> 00:05:17.604
So he gave me an audition and it was for

00:05:17.604 --> 00:05:19.139
this play called Disco Pigs.

00:05:19.139 --> 00:05:20.733
And it became quite successful.

00:05:20.733 --> 00:05:23.983
We made the play then we made the film.

00:05:24.890 --> 00:05:26.640
We man and woman now.

00:05:29.441 --> 00:05:32.101
We lil' barbers no more.

00:05:32.101 --> 00:05:34.416
That kind of changed everything really.

00:05:34.416 --> 00:05:37.760
I had never ever envisaged
ever being in a film

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:39.243
or being on television
or anything like that.

00:05:39.243 --> 00:05:40.880
I wanted to be a theater actor.

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:45.248
That's what I saw happening
when I decided to go for it.

00:05:45.248 --> 00:05:47.208
I moved to London and started to get

00:05:47.208 --> 00:05:48.479
slightly bigger parts.

00:05:48.479 --> 00:05:52.206
I did this film called 28 Days Later.

00:05:52.206 --> 00:05:53.039
Hello!

00:05:55.062 --> 00:05:57.651
People saw that film,
people saw it in America

00:05:57.651 --> 00:05:59.632
and that definitely was a marker as well

00:05:59.632 --> 00:06:02.771
because I was working with Danny Boyle

00:06:02.771 --> 00:06:04.091
and it was the lead part.

00:06:04.091 --> 00:06:06.541
(door creaks open)

00:06:06.541 --> 00:06:07.647
(man gags)

00:06:07.647 --> 00:06:08.602
Father?

00:06:08.602 --> 00:06:10.852
(man gags)

00:06:12.515 --> 00:06:15.515
(bag smacks priest)

00:06:17.282 --> 00:06:18.977
Aw I shouldn't have done that.

00:06:18.977 --> 00:06:21.731
I was really aware of the
script being fantastic

00:06:21.731 --> 00:06:24.779
and Danny being Danny and I really, really

00:06:24.779 --> 00:06:25.722
worked at the auditions.

00:06:25.722 --> 00:06:27.265
I did like five auditions.

00:06:27.265 --> 00:06:29.928
And auditioning is a
terrible, terrible experience

00:06:29.928 --> 00:06:32.140
because you're not giving a performance,

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:34.195
it's a different skill, not
one that I'm very good at

00:06:34.195 --> 00:06:35.608
or was very good at.

00:06:35.608 --> 00:06:38.087
Danny saw something and took a chance

00:06:38.087 --> 00:06:40.127
and that is a film I'm very proud of

00:06:40.127 --> 00:06:41.956
and for some reason it
resonated with people

00:06:41.956 --> 00:06:43.823
and people went to see it.

00:06:43.823 --> 00:06:46.629
So that's where people began to attempt

00:06:46.629 --> 00:06:47.625
to pronounce my name.

00:06:47.625 --> 00:06:49.465
(giggles)

00:06:49.465 --> 00:06:51.729
And have been attempting ever since.

00:06:51.729 --> 00:06:52.858
- [Interviewer] And you're both actors who

00:06:52.858 --> 00:06:57.774
both tend to star in more
dramatic roles than comedic ones.

00:06:57.774 --> 00:06:59.592
Is that a conscious decision as an actor

00:06:59.592 --> 00:07:02.101
or is it literally the
scripts that come in,

00:07:02.101 --> 00:07:06.398
the ones that you like are
the ones with those roles?

00:07:06.398 --> 00:07:08.002
- Yes actually.
- Yeah.

00:07:08.002 --> 00:07:09.019
- It tends to be that.

00:07:09.019 --> 00:07:11.010
I love watching comedies or whatever

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:12.768
but the films that I really, really,

00:07:12.768 --> 00:07:14.494
the ones that are close
to my heart I guess

00:07:14.494 --> 00:07:16.327
are the dramatic ones.

00:07:19.035 --> 00:07:21.169
Generally not just
films, like pieces of art

00:07:21.169 --> 00:07:22.297
and literature and everything,

00:07:22.297 --> 00:07:24.898
they tend to be the ones I think where

00:07:24.898 --> 00:07:25.894
the stakes are raised.

00:07:25.894 --> 00:07:27.926
To me that is drama.

00:07:27.926 --> 00:07:30.586
The greater the stakes,
the greater the drama.

00:07:30.586 --> 00:07:33.127
And that to me is more compelling to watch

00:07:33.127 --> 00:07:34.152
and that's I guess the sort of

00:07:34.152 --> 00:07:36.053
material that I'm drawn to.

00:07:36.053 --> 00:07:39.470
It's always a gamble, that casting thing.

00:07:40.667 --> 00:07:42.987
In fact they do this crazy thing now

00:07:42.987 --> 00:07:44.879
more for boys and girls, they do this

00:07:44.879 --> 00:07:46.626
kind of chemistry test.

00:07:46.626 --> 00:07:48.568
That's the most absurd
thing in the world to me.

00:07:48.568 --> 00:07:50.345
How can you manufacture that?

00:07:50.345 --> 00:07:52.661
It's some weird alchemy, you can't.

00:07:52.661 --> 00:07:55.298
It's just there or it isn't there.

00:07:55.298 --> 00:07:56.131
It was the same for me.

00:07:56.131 --> 00:07:58.228
We went for dinner, it happened that

00:07:58.228 --> 00:08:00.007
myself and my wife were obsessed with

00:08:00.007 --> 00:08:03.592
The Fall at the time and I was like.

00:08:03.592 --> 00:08:05.644
He did such an incredible
job and I love that show

00:08:05.644 --> 00:08:09.272
and then we just met and
it just seemed to work.

00:08:09.272 --> 00:08:10.653
And there's a similar sense of humor

00:08:10.653 --> 00:08:14.679
and a similar approach
to work as Jamie said.

00:08:14.679 --> 00:08:17.512
I forgot how intense that film is,

00:08:19.744 --> 00:08:21.247
it doesn't really let up.

00:08:21.247 --> 00:08:24.397
So we had to, the three of us together,

00:08:24.397 --> 00:08:27.851
we managed to make the atmosphere on set

00:08:27.851 --> 00:08:31.580
quite an enjoyable one
despite everything else.

00:08:31.580 --> 00:08:35.530
And I think again, it's
to Sean's testament.

00:08:35.530 --> 00:08:36.849
That's the wrong word but
Sean should take the credit

00:08:36.849 --> 00:08:38.924
for the fact there was families around

00:08:38.924 --> 00:08:41.626
and it was a very, very lovely environment

00:08:41.626 --> 00:08:44.401
to work in and we had fun.

00:08:44.401 --> 00:08:45.515
And I think you have to.

00:08:45.515 --> 00:08:49.081
You're lucky to be working
on such a good project.

00:08:49.081 --> 00:08:53.625
You can't go around all
sort of sad and intense.

00:08:53.625 --> 00:08:56.205
You've got to get through the day.

00:08:56.205 --> 00:08:58.266
I don't know, sometimes you feel like

00:08:58.266 --> 00:09:00.560
a total waste of space really when

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:02.512
you look at people with proper jobs.

00:09:02.512 --> 00:09:06.941
And then you go, "What am
I doing flouncing around,

00:09:06.941 --> 00:09:08.335
"putting on voices and dressing up?"

00:09:08.335 --> 00:09:11.048
But people need to go to the pictures

00:09:11.048 --> 00:09:13.563
and people need some sort of escape.

00:09:13.563 --> 00:09:15.437
Artists don't let people down like

00:09:15.437 --> 00:09:16.896
politicians let people down,

00:09:16.896 --> 00:09:18.900
bankers let people down.

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:20.598
The quality of the work might fluctuate

00:09:20.598 --> 00:09:22.418
but they keep making it, they keep trying

00:09:22.418 --> 00:09:25.391
and they keep giving some
sort of succor I think.

00:09:25.391 --> 00:09:27.442
Kind of over payed and
there's a huge amount of

00:09:27.442 --> 00:09:30.924
stupidity and hot air
that surrounds it all

00:09:30.924 --> 00:09:33.584
and a lot of that I don't subscribe to

00:09:33.584 --> 00:09:37.618
but I think sometimes a film
can change someone's life.

00:09:37.618 --> 00:09:41.022
If you can do that through
a piece of art then that's

00:09:41.022 --> 00:09:43.237
like you're giving
something important back.

00:09:43.237 --> 00:09:47.118
You have to learn from each
job you do as an actor.

00:09:47.118 --> 00:09:49.991
You're constantly learning and it's an

00:09:49.991 --> 00:09:52.658
ongoing learning process really.

00:09:54.879 --> 00:09:56.655
But this is unique for me certainly

00:09:56.655 --> 00:09:58.420
in having to develop a character

00:09:58.420 --> 00:10:00.597
over a long period of time and sort of

00:10:00.597 --> 00:10:03.900
grow with the character,
I've never done that before.

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:05.708
And I suppose to have
the confidence in going

00:10:05.708 --> 00:10:07.586
that once you've established it

00:10:07.586 --> 00:10:11.169
and people have invested
in it to a degree,

00:10:12.068 --> 00:10:14.248
then you can be brave.

00:10:14.248 --> 00:10:17.298
So I suppose to try to take that from it

00:10:17.298 --> 00:10:19.695
is a thing you can actually
be brave with the character

00:10:19.695 --> 00:10:21.615
and take it places that you mightn't have

00:10:21.615 --> 00:10:25.262
necessarily thought of at the beginning.

00:10:25.262 --> 00:10:29.012
There's such an amount
of resources out there

00:10:31.672 --> 00:10:36.428
for an actor nowadays, you
can research everything.

00:10:36.428 --> 00:10:39.845
And I think that is your duty to do that.

00:10:40.817 --> 00:10:44.067
But again, with every role, I try to do

00:10:45.090 --> 00:10:48.759
as much research as possible,
to have it all available

00:10:48.759 --> 00:10:51.768
and once you've done that,
kind of leave it behind

00:10:51.768 --> 00:10:55.303
because you have to focus on
the truth of the character

00:10:55.303 --> 00:10:56.339
and you have to make the character

00:10:56.339 --> 00:10:58.506
as believable as possible.

00:10:59.468 --> 00:11:02.059
- In terms of research, were there any

00:11:02.059 --> 00:11:04.152
performances that you drew upon?

00:11:04.152 --> 00:11:06.438
Was there any kind of
film or theater or play

00:11:06.438 --> 00:11:07.930
or anything like that that you've watched

00:11:07.930 --> 00:11:10.341
or drew upon to give you
a sense of the character?

00:11:10.341 --> 00:11:13.104
- I don't really go to other performances

00:11:13.104 --> 00:11:15.021
for inspiration really.

00:11:16.778 --> 00:11:19.605
I did a lot of research about soldiers

00:11:19.605 --> 00:11:22.772
that had shell shock and that was what

00:11:24.836 --> 00:11:27.436
sort of informed mostly.

00:11:27.436 --> 00:11:28.808
- On that, what did you do?

00:11:28.808 --> 00:11:32.069
Did you meet with PTSD survivors or?

00:11:32.069 --> 00:11:34.913
- No I didn't, I mean I
wanted to try and sort of,

00:11:34.913 --> 00:11:37.399
because there's a lot about it nowadays

00:11:37.399 --> 00:11:38.593
but back then they didn't have a clue,

00:11:38.593 --> 00:11:41.589
they didn't really know how to treat it

00:11:41.589 --> 00:11:44.596
and the symptoms were so bizarre.

00:11:44.596 --> 00:11:47.910
I read about this one soldier who

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:51.719
actually went blind for
no physiological reason

00:11:51.719 --> 00:11:54.806
other than the trauma made him blind.

00:11:54.806 --> 00:11:56.553
So that was the power
of what they had seen.

00:11:56.553 --> 00:12:00.211
So you kind of have to interpret it

00:12:00.211 --> 00:12:03.794
as sensitively and as
carefully as you can.

00:12:14.332 --> 00:12:16.425
It's very clear that this is a damaged guy

00:12:16.425 --> 00:12:19.260
who's violence is a form
of expression for him.

00:12:19.260 --> 00:12:21.686
That's a big distance to travel for me

00:12:21.686 --> 00:12:23.678
as well to get to that.

00:12:23.678 --> 00:12:27.566
And he's the most sort of
capable person I've ever played.

00:12:27.566 --> 00:12:29.550
And that's not me either.

00:12:29.550 --> 00:12:31.407
- How much did you pay for
the suit you'll be wearing?

00:12:31.407 --> 00:12:33.602
- Oh I don't pay for suits.

00:12:33.602 --> 00:12:36.484
My suits are on the house
or the house burns down.

00:12:36.484 --> 00:12:38.457
You're inhabiting this
character all the time.

00:12:38.457 --> 00:12:40.135
It effects you in a subliminal way.

00:12:40.135 --> 00:12:42.602
It effects you by osmosis.

00:12:42.602 --> 00:12:43.609
But I'm not very aware of it,

00:12:43.609 --> 00:12:44.765
it's only people that are around you

00:12:44.765 --> 00:12:45.955
that are more aware of it.

00:12:45.955 --> 00:12:49.867
Like my wife will be aware of
when I'm playing him I think.

00:12:49.867 --> 00:12:53.707
But I'll come back until
I see (mumbles) were good.

00:12:53.707 --> 00:12:55.109
You have to concentrate a lot.

00:12:55.109 --> 00:12:56.195
And he talks a lot.

00:12:56.195 --> 00:12:57.669
Smokes a lot of fags and is constantly

00:12:57.669 --> 00:13:00.002
fighting so he's exhausting.

00:13:01.233 --> 00:13:04.566
♫ The ceiling is moving

00:13:05.909 --> 00:13:08.659
♫ Moving in time

00:13:09.789 --> 00:13:11.398
I thought it was a terrible
idea at the beginning

00:13:11.398 --> 00:13:13.410
to put contemporary
music on a period show.

00:13:13.410 --> 00:13:14.791
I thought that was silly.

00:13:14.791 --> 00:13:15.880
But then it just seemed to work

00:13:15.880 --> 00:13:17.972
because the artist that they chose were

00:13:17.972 --> 00:13:21.720
these kind of outlaw artists
and that seemed to work.

00:13:21.720 --> 00:13:25.887
('Red Right Hand' by Nick
Cave &amp; The Bad Seeds)

00:13:28.494 --> 00:13:30.670
For me it was huge that
Nick Cave had to watch it

00:13:30.670 --> 00:13:32.999
and say, "Yeah I'll give
you my music for it."

00:13:32.999 --> 00:13:34.044
So the fact that he
had actually watched it

00:13:34.044 --> 00:13:35.877
was a big deal for me.

00:13:38.920 --> 00:13:39.864
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:13:39.864 --> 00:13:43.146
I made this video because
George Sid asked me to.

00:13:43.146 --> 00:13:44.284
If there's someone
you'd like me to profile

00:13:44.284 --> 00:13:46.368
in the future top ten, please check out

00:13:46.368 --> 00:13:48.135
the link down in the description below

00:13:48.135 --> 00:13:51.415
and you can cast your vote
on who we should do next.

00:13:51.415 --> 00:13:53.571
I also want to give a quick
shout out to Pedro Colon.

00:13:53.571 --> 00:13:55.037
Pedro thank you so much for picking up

00:13:55.037 --> 00:13:58.737
the audio version of
my book, Your One Word.

00:13:58.737 --> 00:14:00.151
You said that you didn't expect it to be

00:14:00.151 --> 00:14:02.060
such an amazing book so I'm glad we were

00:14:02.060 --> 00:14:04.853
able to over deliver
on those expectations.

00:14:04.853 --> 00:14:06.368
But I really appreciate you sharing that

00:14:06.368 --> 00:14:08.156
on Twitter with your audience as well.

00:14:08.156 --> 00:14:09.273
It really means a lot to me and

00:14:09.273 --> 00:14:10.592
I'm glad you enjoyed the book.

00:14:10.592 --> 00:14:11.894
Thank you guys again for watching.

00:14:11.894 --> 00:14:13.032
I believe in you.

00:14:13.032 --> 00:14:15.052
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:14:15.052 --> 00:14:17.935
and whatever your one word is, much love,

00:14:17.935 --> 00:14:19.644
I'll see you soon.

00:14:19.644 --> 00:14:21.921
(air swooshes)

